{"authored" [{"question" "q-129459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039987/state.json" "file_sha256" "c214e034109a4d35a776f90289590cb5ec56b86ce4f2b3ad3b814645b045b53f" "basis" 1400 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+3z=-2 \\\\  & y+4z=0 \\\\  & z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+4(3)=0\\,⟹\\,y=-12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-12$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-12)+3(3)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-2+12-9=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1$, $y=-12$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨1,-12,3⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039987/state.json" "file_sha256" "c214e034109a4d35a776f90289590cb5ec56b86ce4f2b3ad3b814645b045b53f" "basis" 1400 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039987/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039987/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cdfdf77afed68f2fd83e2d020aaa86